# Where the Sun, the Moon and the planets are, as seen from the Earth: each one's ecliptic longitude, of the date's equinox, in degrees. Rewritten in Hazel from astronomia (https://github.com/commenthol/astronomia, MIT), Sonia Keys's and commenthol's port of Jean Meeus's Astronomical Algorithms (2nd ed., 1998): the Sun as its solar.js has it (Meeus 25), the Moon as its moonposition.js (Meeus 47, the longitude's 60 terms), the planets from their mean orbital elements of date (planetelements.js, Meeus 31) by Kepler's equation, and Pluto from its periodic terms (pluto.js, Meeus 37). The tables below are made by tools/make-sky-tables.py from astronomia's source. The time is taken as Universal Time throughout: the difference from the dynamical time Meeus means, about a minute today, moves the Moon about 0.01 degrees. #
# ---- tables #
